welcome to the Malden district council  webinar working safely in shops and  branches a basic guide to the new  working practices my name is Damien  Geller and I'm the lead specialist for  community at Northern District Council  who is this guidance for those working  in the retail and service sector such as  employers employees and the  self-employed the government sets out  five tests which if met will allow some  businesses to reopen to the public from  the 15th of June 2020 to help businesses  reopen safely the government provided  new information on how they should  operate when delivering services these  eight guides are there to help  businesses adapt to new ways of working  and consider how to implement them as  business owners it is up to you to  choose the best guide or combination of  guides for your business please note  that this guidance does not apply to the  hospitality sector and at this stage you  are still required to be closed once you  have decided to reopen your business the  government advice is for business owners  to carry out a risk assessment making  sure to consider the core and essential  functions of your business  Kenya services be delivered from home or  online rather than from site how many  staff are needed to operate the business  at a basic level  are there any functions that can be  suspended until things return to a more  normal situation do you have staff with  disabilities or protected  characteristics or special circumstances  that make them more vulnerable any  premises adaptations increased premises  clean regimes or protective equipment  please remember to share any risk  assessments you have made with your  workforce caring for your staff  encouraging social distancing as an  employer you have a legal duty to  provide a safe working environment for  employees when considering how to adapt  your operating arrangements  please remember to consider the  requirements of the equality and  diversity Act 2010 employers can  consider such things as offering  guidance and support on traveling to and  from work safely adopting hygiene  processes for staff when arriving  or leaving the workplace increased  availability of hand-washing and hygiene  facilities for use throughout the day  the use of fixed teams to minimize the  opportunity for virus transmission  adapting work benches or workstations to  promote side-to-side or back to back  working the installation of protective  barriers for staff stopping the shared  use of general consumables such as pens  or writing pads the suspension of hot  desking arrangements and/or the  increased of cleaning regimes for your  premises caring for your customers  encouraging social distancing as a  business you must consider the safety of  customers wishing to access your  premises when considering how to adapt  your business please remember to  consider the requirements of the  equality and diversity Act 2010 safe  accommodation of customers can include  such things as the increased  availability of hand-washing and hygiene  facilities for customer use controlling  the number of customers able to enter  your premises adapting your shop floor  by using a one-way system or widening  aisles increased cleaning regimes for  premises including changing rooms if  they are essential for the function of  the business increase cleaning for key  touch points such as door handles or  contactless payments increased cleaning  of high touch display items providing  counter services by the shop entrance to  reduce the need for customers to enter  the shop to buy goods and encouraging  the use of contactless forms of payment  continuing to care for your customers  encouraging social distancing answers to  some questions from business owners  within the Malden district can a cafe  place tables and chairs on their  pavement outside of their premises and  spaced two meters apart know as the  pavement space will be prioritized for  pedestrians unless by agreement with the  council and where the pavement has been  clearly marked as forming part of the  business are shops allowed to mark the  pavement for queueing customers again  though highway laws prevent the pavement  from being indelibly marked you may  affixed signage within your own windows  or on the outside of your prime  but please note that a boards are not  allowed can other shop queues pass the  shop boundary yes provided the queues do  not impact or block the flow of  pedestrians using the footpath if this  is a risk then marshalling may be needed  is it safe to use aircon units possibly  the science suggests that where possible  airflow should be fresh and not  recirculated can changing room  facilities be used for non-essential  clothing types such as proms or wedding  dresses yes but only if it is essential  to the function of the business and with  increased cleaning provisions caring for  your stock control the virus as a  functioning business you will have  regular deliveries pickups and turnover  of stock and consumables depending on  your service you may also have to deal  with returns of unwanted or faulty items  in order to improve safety within your  workplace you may wish to consider such  things as setting aside a dedicated room  or container for delivered items to be  stored safely for 72 hours before  handling arranging for returned items to  be cleaned and stored for 72 hours prior  to being placed back on display and  making arrangements for pickups to be  made from easy to access parts of your  premises thereby reducing the need of  couriers to enter your building  minimizing transmission control the  virus as a functioning business you will  have regular cleaning regimes for your  premises already in place the new  guidance places greater emphasis on  regular cleaning and methods for  lessening the chance of virus  transmission many instances businesses  will need new products or facilities to  help them make their businesses safe so  you may wish to consider such things as  installing screens to shield both staff  and customers who are moving around the  premises encourage the use of face masks  and gloves within your store installing  new signage which can guide customers  seeking to access your services  installing hygiene and extra sanitation  stations for customer use and consider  the use of online ordering or click and  Collect services accessing information  saving lives please remember every  business is different and this  information is for guidance only having  been adapted from various online sources  this advice is only applicable to  England and will be updated over time  you can find additional information and  support by accessing the following links  below  official government guidance can be  found by following WWE UK guidance /  working safely during coronavirus kovat  19 business support UK also offers a  range of reference videos and webinars  which can be found by following the  youtube link below and lastly thank you  for all your hard work and all the  services that you provide to the  community be safe
 

